Practical 4: Connecting TikTok to PostgreSQL with Prisma ORM

This project demonstrates how to migrate a TikTok clone backend from in-memory data storage to a fully persistent PostgreSQL database using Prisma ORM. It also implements authentication using JWT and password encryption with bcrypt, while upgrading all REST API endpoints to work with real database models.

The goal of this practical is to understand database integration, schema design, authentication, and how modern ORMs simplify backend development by replacing raw SQL with structured and type-safe queries.

Project Objectives
Set up and configure a PostgreSQL database
Integrate Prisma ORM with the backend
Replace in-memory data with persistent database storage
Implement secure authentication using JWT and bcrypt
Update REST API endpoints to use Prisma instead of local models
Seed database with test data for development
Part 1: PostgreSQL Setup
Create Database

A PostgreSQL database is created for the application:

Database name: tiktok_db
User: tiktok_user
Password-based authentication enabled

This database will store all application data including users, videos, comments, likes, and follow relationships.

Part 2: Prisma ORM Setup
Install Dependencies
npm install @prisma/client
npm install prisma --save-dev
Initialize Prisma
npx prisma init

This creates:

prisma/schema.prisma (database schema)
.env file (environment configuration)
Configure Database Connection

The .env file includes:

PostgreSQL connection string
Database credentials
Schema configuration
Install Authentication Libraries
npm install bcrypt jsonwebtoken
Part 3: Prisma Schema & Migration
Define Database Schema

The Prisma schema defines:

Users
Videos
Comments
Likes
Follows

It also defines relationships such as:

One-to-many (User → Videos, Comments)
Many-to-many (Likes, Follows)
Run Migration
npx prisma migrate dev --name init

This command:

Creates SQL migration files
Applies schema to PostgreSQL
Generates Prisma Client
Prisma Client Setup

A reusable Prisma instance is created in:

src/lib/prisma.js

This allows database access throughout the application.

Part 4: Authentication System
JWT Authentication

Authentication is implemented using:

bcrypt → password hashing
jsonwebtoken → token generation
middleware → route protection
Auth Middleware

The middleware:

Verifies JWT tokens
Protects private routes
Attaches user data to requests
Part 5: Updating Controllers with Prisma

All controllers are updated to replace in-memory logic with database queries.

User Controller
User registration with hashed passwords
Login with JWT token generation
Fetch users from database
Video Controller
Create and retrieve videos
Fetch related user data
Handle likes and aggregations
Support relational queries
Comment Controller
Create, update, delete comments
Handle comment likes
Link comments to users and videos
Route Protection

Protected routes now require valid JWT tokens before allowing:

Video creation
Likes
Follow actions
Comment operations
Part 6: Environment Configuration

The .env file includes:

PORT=5000
NODE_ENV=development

DATABASE_URL="postgresql://tiktok_user:your_password@localhost:5432/tiktok_db?schema=public"

JWT_SECRET=yourverylongandsecurerandomsecret
JWT_EXPIRE=30d
Part 7: Database Seeding
Seed Script

A seed script is created to populate the database with test data.

It generates:

10 users
50 videos
200 comments
300 video likes
150 comment likes
40 follow relationships
Run Seed Script
npm run seed
Part 8: Testing the System
Start Server
npm run dev
Test Authentication

Using Postman:

Register new user
Login user
Copy JWT token
Test Protected Routes

Use token to access:

Create video
Like video
Comment on video
Follow users
Key Concepts
1. Database Schema Design
Tables represent real-world entities
Relationships define data connections
Foreign keys ensure integrity
Indexes improve performance
2. ORM (Prisma)

Prisma provides:

Type-safe database queries
Easy schema migrations
Relationship handling
Reduced SQL complexity
3. Authentication

Security is implemented using:

Password hashing (bcrypt)
JWT tokens
Middleware-based route protection
4. Prisma Features
Schema-based modeling
Automatic migrations
Query builder abstraction
Relationship-aware queries
Transaction support